WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.580
Nothing racks up the ticket sales quite like
a "based on a true story" tagline.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:08.471
But as we all know, Hollywood’s version
of the truth doesn’t always add up to the

00:00:08.471 --> 00:00:09.670
actual fact.

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:14.270
Sure, crazy things happen in real life all
the time, but screenwriters still can’t

00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:18.320
seem to resist juicing up the story to make
things better — and bigger.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:23.359
"Evolution has programmed our fabulous male
brains to take anything anybody else thinks

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:25.240
is important and make it bigger!"

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.020
So what’s real and what isn’t?

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:33.760
Here’s a look at some "true story" movies
that were anything but.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:35.410
The Revenant

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:41.350
Historical fact checkers had a field day with
Alejandro G. Iñárritu's Oscar-winning 2015

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:42.450
film The Revenant.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:47.400
While Hugh Glass, played by Leonardo DiCaprio,
was a real frontiersman who survived some

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:52.270
perilous obstacles — yes, even a bear fight
— there were still plenty of made-up moments

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:54.470
the movie added for dramatic flair.

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:58.760
According to historical record, Glass did
not have a son, so the movie's entire plot

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:03.410
about him seeking vengeance against the men
who killed his kid was completely fabricated.

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:07.789
And while Glass did go after John Fitzgerald
— played by Tom Hardy — for leaving him

00:01:07.789 --> 00:01:12.039
to die in the woods, Glass never actually
got the opportunity to have a face-to-face

00:01:12.039 --> 00:01:13.039
showdown like he did in the movie.

00:01:13.039 --> 00:01:18.799
Instead, he gave up his search after finding
out that Fitzgerald had become an Army member,

00:01:18.799 --> 00:01:21.100
and was thus protected by the federal government.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:22.540
Talk about anticlimactic.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.280
"Aw man, you're more of a letdown than Fruit
Stripe gum."

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.560
The Walk

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:37.280
Let's just get this out of the way: yes, Philippe
Petit's nail-biting tightrope walk between

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:42.420
the two towers of the World Trade Center was
real, and many of the scenes and details depicted

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:43.759
in The Walk were true.

00:01:43.759 --> 00:01:48.149
That includes Petit's lack of a harness or
a safety net, his previous walk between the

00:01:48.149 --> 00:01:53.109
towers of Notre Dame in 1971, and the buildings'
natural sway in the wind.

00:01:53.109 --> 00:01:57.069
And Petit really was arrested for the stunt,
but the charges were dropped as long as he

00:01:57.069 --> 00:01:59.100
did a free performance at Central Park.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:04.789
But some of the details were, well, rearranged
to suit the pace and personality of the film.

00:02:04.789 --> 00:02:09.280
Petit and his team actually had to do three
trips to New York to prepare, instead of the

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:10.890
one shown in the flick.

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:15.420
And according to Bustle, in real life Petit
hooked up with a groupie after being released

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:20.750
by police, leading to his breakup with Annie
Allix, who in the movie was played by Charlotte

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:21.750
Le Bon.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.910
Cheating on the lady who flew across the world
just to help you achieve your goals?

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:28.220
That's a whole different kind of tightrope
walk.

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:30.210
The Texas Chainsaw Massacre

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:35.640
Tobe Hooper's groundbreaking 1974 low-budget
horror film The Texas Chainsaw Massacre may

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:39.170
have been advertised as a true story, but
it's far from it.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:43.110
While Leatherface is arguably on the Mount
Rushmore of famous horror movie villains,

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:47.760
he really didn't get his sadistic swag from
a real-life chainsaw-wielding maniac.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.550
Like Psycho's Norman Bates and countless other
creepybad guys...

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:56.550
"It rubs the lotion on its skin or else it
gets the hose again."

00:02:56.550 --> 00:03:00.280
...Leatherface was loosely based on the real-life
killer Ed Gein.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:05.070
Gein, who confessed to killing two women,
was known for exhuming corpses from graveyards

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:08.140
and making keepsakes and furniture out of
their remains.

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:12.730
While these actions heavily influenced the
creation of Leatherface, his family, and the

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:16.850
decor of the Sawyers' household, that's about
it when it comes to The Texas Chainsaw Massacre

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:18.930
being a true story.

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:20.920
Cinderella Man

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:26.640
Ron Howard's 2005 biopic Cinderella Man wasn't
too off the mark when it came to the central

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:31.400
story of boxer James J. Braddock, a heavyweight
champ who came back from bitter defeat to

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.160
win the belt from Max Baer.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:37.920
However, the movie also turned Braddock’s
in-the-ring nemesis into an over-the-top cinematic

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.840
villain — nothing like Baer was in real
life.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:45.330
While it's true that Baer accidentally killed
opponent Frankie Campbell in the ring, Baer’s

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:49.760
son, Max Baer, Jr., was reportedly enraged
by the scoundrel his father was portrayed

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:50.760
to be in the movie.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:57.060
He said, "the portrayal of my father in Cinderella
Man couldn't have been more wrong and inaccurate.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:01.060
They turned a good-hearted, fun-loving, friendly
and warm human being who hated boxing into

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:04.510
Mr. T from Rocky III with no redeeming characteristics."

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:09.490
"No I don’t hate Balboa, but I pity the
fool, and I will destroy any man who tries

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:11.540
to take what I got."

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:15.319
Baer himself was once was quoted as saying,
"Nothing that ever happened to me — nothing

00:04:15.319 --> 00:04:19.220
that can happen to me — affected me like
the death of Frankie Campbell."

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:23.650
He ended up raising money for Campbell's widow
and son, and claimed to have nightmares about

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:25.040
the man’s death.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:26.970
The Fourth Kind

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:32.120
The 2009 sci-fi thriller The Fourth Kind was
marketed as the true story of the mysterious

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:36.010
disappearance of several people from Nome,
Alaska in October 2000.

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:39.430
"Every scene in this movie is supported by
archived footage.

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:43.540
Some of what you're about to see is extremely
disturbing."

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:48.340
The mayor of the town, Denise Michels, cautioned
that the supposed true story that inspired

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:52.750
the movie is quite different than what was
shown, and this has caused her town's officials

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:56.290
some immense headaches from truth-hunters
who called in after the film's release.

00:04:56.290 --> 00:05:00.570
Michels told CNN, "People need to realize
that this is a science fiction thriller.

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:04.150
Some of the calls I'm just ignoring because
the issue we had to deal with in real life

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:05.320
was very sensitive."

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:11.320
What really happened was a string of traveler
disappearances in 2005, which the FBI investigated

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:16.290
and determined was caused by a combination
of alcohol consumption and freezing temperatures

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:17.290
in the area.

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:21.740
The fact that the movie poised itself as a
combination of actual documentary footage,

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:26.370
spliced together with mere dramatizations
of the same, was a convincing seat-filler

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:29.610
in the vein of The Blair Witch Project and
Paranormal Activity.

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:33.810
But the movie was ultimately labeled a hoax
for its blatant misrepresentations of the

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:34.810
truth.

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:36.810
Not that anybody was really surprised.

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:38.910
Catch Me If You Can

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:43.850
Steven Spielberg's 2002 caper movie Catch
Me If You Can wouldn't have been the same

00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:48.331
without the thrilling game of cat and mouse
that played out between Tom Hanks' FBI agent

00:05:48.331 --> 00:05:53.350
Carl Hanratty and Leonardo DiCaprio's version
of the real-life Frank Abagnale.

00:05:53.350 --> 00:05:57.520
But if the movie had really echoed reality,
Tom Hanks wouldn't have been a part of it

00:05:57.520 --> 00:05:58.520
at all.

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:03.040
According to USA Today, Hanks' character was
actually a combination of multiple agents

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:06.520
who'd followed Abagnale through his many careers
in con artistry.

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:10.930
One of the first scams Abagnale pulled off
in the movie was a fabrication built just

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:15.610
for the big screen: namely when Abagnale,
a high schooler himself, convinces a class

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:17.750
that he's their substitute French teacher.

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:19.230
That didn't happen.

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:23.330
Abagnale also called shenanigans on the scene
in which DiCaprio's character calls up his

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:27.740
nemesis in the FBI out of sheer desperation
and loneliness on Christmas Eve.

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:29.669
He asked, "Why would I do that?

00:06:29.669 --> 00:06:32.610
I didn't want the FBI to know where I was."

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:34.760
Good Morning Vietnam

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:39.640
The real-life account of Adrian Cronauer's
time as a plucky disc jockey during the Vietnam

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:41.280
War is plenty intriguing.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:45.750
But when Robin Williams stepped in to portray
Cronauer in Good Morning Vietnam, things took

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:47.640
a definite turn towards left field.

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:48.640
"Let's try something.

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:50.920
Let's play this backwards and see if it gets
any better…"

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:01.740
The real Cronauer admitted at the American
Veterans Center's 2008 conference that he

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:04.699
wasn't quite as kooky in reality as his onscreen
persona:

00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:10.300
"Well, those of you who had been in the military
know, if I had done half of the stuff Robin

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:15.030
Williams did in that movie, I'd still be in
Leavenworth this morning instead of Washington."

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:19.040
Among the major jumps from reality was the
fact that Cronauer wasn't actually kicked

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:24.840
out of Vietnam as a result of his on-air antics,
and most of the lines were ad-libbed by Williams.

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:28.820
Cronauer admitted, "Williams is the disc jockey
I would have liked to be …The movie is much

00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:32.700
more interesting than the experiences I had…Robin
Williams is very funny.

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:33.700
I'm not."

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:36.220
Still, the most famous line in the movie…

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:39.660
"Gooooooood morning, Vietnam!"

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:41.840
...came directly from Cronauer.

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:43.830
So at least the title of the movie's accurate.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:45.229
Thanks for watching!

00:07:45.229 --> 00:07:48.030
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:50.360
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

